          src: "/diagrams/NG-CLP-2851.svg",
          alt: "Infusion rates: two formulas and the sense-check. Drops per minute for a gravity set equals volume in millilitres times the drop factor, divided by the time in minutes; standard adult set 20 drops per millilitre, blood set 15, paediatric burette 60. Millilitres per hour for a pump equals total volume divided by hours; 1,000 millilitres over 8 hours is 125 millilitres per hour. Worked example, 1,000 millilitres over 8 hours with a 20-drop set: 8 times 60 is 480 minutes; 1,000 times 20 is 20,000; 20,000 divided by 480 is 41.7, so about 42 drops per minute; the sense-check is 125 millilitres an hour, roughly 2 millilitres a minute, about 40 drops. Decimal rules: always a leading zero, write 0.5 milligrams never point 5; never a trailing zero, write 5 milligrams never 5.0; write micrograms in full. If the answer needs more than a tablet and a half, more than 2 millilitres intramuscularly, or looks surprising, stop and recalculate.",
