          src: "/diagrams/NG-DRC-2431.svg",
          alt: "The intravenous line: what goes wrong and how it looks. Infiltration, fluid into the tissue: swelling, cool pale skin, infusion slows or stops, no blood return — stop, remove, elevate, resite the other arm. Phlebitis, the vein is inflamed: redness tracking along the vein, warm, tender, a palpable cord — stop, remove, warm compress, document. Cool means infiltration, warm means phlebitis. Extravasation, a vesicant leaks out: same signs as infiltration but the drug destroys tissue — cytotoxics, calcium, potassium, vasopressors — do not remove the cannula. Extravasation of a vesicant, the order: stop the infusion immediately; leave the cannula in and aspirate back; call for senior and antidote advice; elevate, mark the area, photograph, document. Pulling the cannula first throws away your only way of aspirating the drug. Air embolism: sudden breathlessness, chest pain, a churning sound at the heart, collapse — clamp the line, turn the patient onto the left side with the head down, give oxygen, call. Potassium chloride, the one that kills quietly: never as an intravenous push; peripheral infusion should not normally exceed 10 millimoles an hour and the bag must be inverted.",
